1
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,660
I'd been driving for
almost 11 hours when the

2
00:00:14,660 --> 00:00:16,500
detour signs pushed me off the interstate.

3
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:20,000
The road narrowed to two
lanes, then the radio

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,340
lost its station, and for a long time the

5
00:00:22,340 --> 00:00:24,140
only light out there
was my own headlights.

6
00:00:25,220 --> 00:00:28,860
Around two in the morning, I saw a gas
station sign glowing over the fields.

7
00:00:29,540 --> 00:00:34,060
The pumps were on, the store was dark, and
there was one other car parked by the air

8
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:36,860
machine with its engine
running and nobody inside.

9
00:00:37,740 --> 00:00:40,160
This was in October, about six years ago.

10
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:45,020
I was 24 and moving back home to Missouri
after a job in Colorado fell apart.

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,380
Everything I owned was in the backseat
of a Corolla with a cracked windshield.

12
00:00:49,980 --> 00:00:52,220
I'd planned to stop for the
night in a motel somewhere

13
00:00:52,220 --> 00:00:54,700
in western Kansas, but I kept telling

14
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:57,820
myself one more hour, and
by the time the detour

15
00:00:57,820 --> 00:00:59,720
came up, I was too tired to be smart

16
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:00,500
about it.

17
00:01:00,940 --> 00:01:03,340
I pulled up to the pump,
farthest from the other car.

18
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,940
It was an older station wagon, dark green
with wood panels on the sides and a bumper

19
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:10,100
sticker that said, Proud
Parent of an Honor Student,

20
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:11,500
with the name of the school scratched

21
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:12,300
off.

22
00:01:12,420 --> 00:01:14,300
The exhaust was rising in the cold.

23
00:01:14,760 --> 00:01:16,700
The headlights were off,
but the dome light was

24
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:18,800
on, and I could see a
jacket on the passenger

25
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:20,760
seat and a fast food bag on the dashboard.

26
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:23,780
I figured whoever it
belonged to was in the

27
00:01:23,780 --> 00:01:24,700
bathroom around the side of the building.

28
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:27,000
I filled the tank and waited.

29
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:28,340
I don't know why I waited.

30
00:01:28,760 --> 00:01:32,680
I think I wanted to see someone walk back
to that car, just so I could stop thinking

31
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:33,480
about it.

32
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:34,940
Nobody came.

33
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:37,440
The pump clicked off,
the screen asked if I

34
00:01:37,440 --> 00:01:39,760
wanted a receipt, and the whole time that

35
00:01:39,760 --> 00:01:43,360
engine kept idling, steady, like
it had been running for hours.

36
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:45,720
I didn't go check the bathroom.

37
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:47,700
I got back in my car and left.

38
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:52,420
About twenty minutes later, the
detour signs ended at a truck stop.

39
00:01:52,720 --> 00:01:55,640
It was the first real building
I'd seen in a long time.

40
00:01:56,240 --> 00:01:58,760
A diner on one side, a
couple of semis parked

41
00:01:58,760 --> 00:02:00,940
out back with their running lights on, and

42
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:04,700
a sign that said, Open 24 hours,
with two of the letters burned out.

43
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:07,660
I parked near the
entrance because I needed

44
00:02:07,660 --> 00:02:09,880
coffee, and I needed to sit somewhere with

45
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:10,740
people for a while.

46
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:15,620
Inside, it was warm and smelled
like burnt coffee and bacon grease.

47
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:18,020
There was a trucker at
the counter, reading

48
00:02:18,020 --> 00:02:20,460
his phone, an older man asleep in a booth

49
00:02:20,460 --> 00:02:21,880
by the window, and one waitress.

50
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:25,480
Her name tag said D.
She looked about fifty,

51
00:02:25,820 --> 00:02:27,740
and she called me Honey
before I'd even sat

52
00:02:27,740 --> 00:02:28,540
down.

53
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:31,680
I took a booth facing
the door, ordered coffee

54
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:33,700
and a slice of pie, and started looking at

55
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:38,040
the map on my phone, trying to figure
out how far I was from anything.

56
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:41,460
That's when the couple came in.

57
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:44,000
They were young, maybe my age.

58
00:02:44,780 --> 00:02:46,960
The guy wore a gray
hoodie, and the girl had

59
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:49,120
on a denim jacket that
was too thin for the

60
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:49,920
weather.

61
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:52,140
It had started to rain
outside, and the shoulders

62
00:02:52,140 --> 00:02:53,560
of their jackets were dark with it.

63
00:02:54,320 --> 00:02:57,520
They were arguing in that low,
tight way people argue in public.

64
00:02:58,380 --> 00:03:00,200
As they walked past my
booth, the girl said,

65
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:01,620
Every single time, Ray.

66
00:03:01,860 --> 00:03:02,960
Every single time.

67
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:04,560
He didn't answer.

68
00:03:05,240 --> 00:03:07,580
They sat two booths down
from me, across the

69
00:03:07,580 --> 00:03:09,980
aisle, and D. went over with menus and two

70
00:03:09,980 --> 00:03:11,060
waters like it was nothing.

71
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:13,480
I didn't think much about it.

72
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:14,960
I ate my pie.

73
00:03:15,540 --> 00:03:17,900
I heard bits of their
conversation, something about

74
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:20,260
a wrong turn and whose idea it had been

75
00:03:20,260 --> 00:03:21,140
to leave so late.

76
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:24,480
The girl had a laugh that
wasn't really a laugh.

77
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:26,900
At one point, Ray said, We'll be fine.

78
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:28,100
We're almost there.

79
00:03:28,720 --> 00:03:29,740
She didn't say anything back.

80
00:03:30,620 --> 00:03:32,640
After a while, I got up
to use the restroom.

81
00:03:32,940 --> 00:03:35,220
It was at the end of a short
hallway past the kitchen.

82
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:37,520
I remember the hallway
because one of the fluorescent

83
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:39,020
lights was flickering, and there

84
00:03:39,020 --> 00:03:42,340
was a corkboard on the wall with business
cards and a flyer for a missing dog.

85
00:03:42,860 --> 00:03:45,060
I was in there maybe
three or four minutes.

86
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:47,900
I washed my hands,
splashed water on my face,

87
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:50,080
and looked at myself in
the mirror, thinking

88
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:51,620
I really needed to find a bed.

89
00:03:52,860 --> 00:03:56,880
When I came out and walked back toward my
booth, the bell over the front door rang.

90
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,580
It was them, the same
couple, gray hoodie, thin

91
00:04:01,580 --> 00:04:03,940
denim jacket, the
shoulders of both jackets

92
00:04:03,940 --> 00:04:04,740
dark with rain.

93
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:07,680
They walked in the same
way, him a step ahead,

94
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:09,720
her a little behind with her arms crossed,

95
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:15,520
and as they passed me, the girl said,
Every single time, Ray, every single time.

96
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:17,500
I stopped in the aisle.

97
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:20,740
I looked over at the booth two down
from mine, where they'd been sitting.

98
00:04:21,160 --> 00:04:22,040
It was empty.

99
00:04:22,860 --> 00:04:25,760
No menus, no glasses, no
wet rings on the table.

100
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:28,660
It had been wiped down like
nobody had used it all night.

101
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:31,100
They sat in that same booth.

102
00:04:31,940 --> 00:04:34,440
Dee came over with two
menus and two waters, and

103
00:04:34,440 --> 00:04:36,900
she smiled at them, and she said, Evening

104
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:37,700
you two.

105
00:04:37,780 --> 00:04:38,620
Rough night out there?

106
00:04:39,120 --> 00:04:41,300
Exactly how you'd greet
someone who just walked in.

107
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:45,000
I sat down because I didn't
know what else to do.

108
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:46,740
My coffee was still there.

109
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:47,760
My plate was still there.

110
00:04:48,280 --> 00:04:49,340
I watched them.

111
00:04:49,820 --> 00:04:52,020
I kept waiting for one
of them to look at me,

112
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:54,260
or laugh, or for this to turn into some

113
00:04:54,260 --> 00:04:55,060
kind of joke.

114
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:56,980
Ray was rubbing his eyes.

115
00:04:57,540 --> 00:04:59,680
The girl was looking out the
window at the parking lot.

116
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:02,480
They were having the same conversation.

117
00:05:03,260 --> 00:05:04,060
Wrong turn.

118
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:05,180
Leaving too late.

119
00:05:05,240 --> 00:05:07,080
And then Ray said, We'll be fine.

120
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:08,100
We're almost there.

121
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:10,320
I looked at the trucker at the counter.

122
00:05:10,820 --> 00:05:12,880
He hadn't looked up from
his phone the whole time.

123
00:05:13,740 --> 00:05:15,740
The old man in the window
booth was still asleep.

124
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:19,140
Nobody else in that room seemed
to think anything was wrong.

125
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:22,200
I left a twenty on the
table and walked out.

126
00:05:22,580 --> 00:05:23,480
I didn't wait for change.

127
00:05:23,820 --> 00:05:27,300
When I got outside, the rain had
stopped, and the parking lot was dry.

128
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:28,500
Completely dry.

129
00:05:29,080 --> 00:05:29,880
No puddles.

130
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:31,120
Nothing wet on my windshield.

131
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:34,440
I stood there for a second,
looking at the ground.

132
00:05:34,920 --> 00:05:37,240
Then I got in my car,
locked the doors, and sat

133
00:05:37,240 --> 00:05:39,140
with the engine running for a long time.

134
00:05:40,680 --> 00:05:43,000
I didn't want to drive past
that gas station again.

135
00:05:43,720 --> 00:05:45,280
I didn't want to go back inside, either.

136
00:05:45,460 --> 00:05:48,260
So I just sat there
until the sky started to

137
00:05:48,260 --> 00:05:50,260
turn gray, and then I followed the signs

138
00:05:50,260 --> 00:05:51,060
back to the interstate.

139
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:54,400
I didn't pass the gas
station on the way back.

140
00:05:54,940 --> 00:05:56,140
I took the same road.

141
00:05:56,180 --> 00:05:56,980
I'm sure of that.

142
00:05:57,580 --> 00:05:58,500
It just wasn't there.

143
00:05:59,380 --> 00:06:02,340
I had a dash cam back then,
one of those cheap ones

144
00:06:02,340 --> 00:06:04,160
that records over itself every few hours.

145
00:06:05,060 --> 00:06:07,560
When I finally got home
and checked it, most

146
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:08,860
of that night had
already been overwritten.

147
00:06:09,680 --> 00:06:12,440
The only file left starts
as I pull into the truck

148
00:06:12,440 --> 00:06:14,420
stop parking lot at 2.14 in the morning.

149
00:06:15,320 --> 00:06:18,000
I park, the camera keeps
recording, and about

150
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:20,220
a minute later, the dark
green station wagon

151
00:06:20,220 --> 00:06:22,160
pulls into the spot right in front of me.

152
00:06:22,360 --> 00:06:25,500
Engine running, dome
light on, nobody inside.

153
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:29,220
The date on that clip is October 14th.

154
00:06:29,580 --> 00:06:32,100
The year is the one before
I ever drove that road.

155
00:06:32,780 --> 00:06:35,620
I still drive that interstate
a couple of times a year.

156
00:06:36,380 --> 00:06:38,660
If the detour signs are
up, I don't take them.

157
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:41,700
I find the last town
before the exit, I park

158
00:06:41,700 --> 00:06:43,960
somewhere well lit, and I sleep in my car

159
00:06:43,960 --> 00:06:44,760
until morning.

160
00:06:52,708 --> 00:06:55,028
I ran out of gas on a
state highway in Nevada

161
00:06:55,028 --> 00:06:57,868
a little after 10 at night, about 40 miles

162
00:06:57,868 --> 00:06:58,668
from the nearest town.

163
00:06:59,348 --> 00:07:00,308
That part was my fault.

164
00:07:00,588 --> 00:07:03,468
I was 22, driving from
Reno to see my sister in

165
00:07:03,468 --> 00:07:05,308
Arizona, and I'd skipped the last station

166
00:07:05,308 --> 00:07:08,528
because the prices were ridiculous and
I was sure there'd be another one soon.

167
00:07:08,988 --> 00:07:09,788
There wasn't.

168
00:07:10,408 --> 00:07:13,908
The car started sputtering
on a long straight stretch

169
00:07:13,908 --> 00:07:16,228
with nothing on either side but sagebrush

170
00:07:16,228 --> 00:07:17,048
and dark hills.

171
00:07:17,048 --> 00:07:20,268
I coasted onto the shoulder
and sat there with

172
00:07:20,268 --> 00:07:22,468
my hazards on, listening to the engine

173
00:07:22,468 --> 00:07:23,568
tick as it cooled.

174
00:07:24,188 --> 00:07:25,868
I had one bar of signal.

175
00:07:26,508 --> 00:07:28,908
I called roadside
assistance, gave them the

176
00:07:28,908 --> 00:07:31,288
mile marker from my map app, and the woman

177
00:07:31,288 --> 00:07:34,048
on the phone told me a tow truck
would be there in about 90 minutes.

178
00:07:35,068 --> 00:07:36,248
She asked if I felt safe.

179
00:07:36,748 --> 00:07:39,628
I said, yes, because what
else are you supposed to say?

180
00:07:40,368 --> 00:07:43,348
The first thing I noticed
was how quiet it was.

181
00:07:43,348 --> 00:07:46,528
When the engine stopped
ticking, there was nothing.

182
00:07:47,348 --> 00:07:49,968
No wind, no insects, no traffic.

183
00:07:50,288 --> 00:07:51,868
I could hear my own breathing.

184
00:07:52,928 --> 00:07:54,728
Every few minutes, I'd
turn the key just far

185
00:07:54,728 --> 00:07:56,248
enough to check the time on the dashboard,

186
00:07:56,628 --> 00:07:58,328
then turned it off again
to save the battery.

187
00:07:58,688 --> 00:08:03,628
I had half a bottle of water and a bag of
sunflower seeds, and I remember rationing

188
00:08:03,628 --> 00:08:06,808
the water like I was in a survival
movie, which is funny now.

189
00:08:07,728 --> 00:08:10,828
About thirty minutes in,
headlights came up behind me.

190
00:08:10,828 --> 00:08:14,068
My stomach dropped, but then I
saw the light bar on the roof.

191
00:08:14,348 --> 00:08:15,568
It was a sheriff's deputy.

192
00:08:16,088 --> 00:08:18,468
He pulled in behind me and
walked up with a flashlight,

193
00:08:18,548 --> 00:08:20,788
an older guy with a gray mustache

194
00:08:20,788 --> 00:08:22,248
and a tired, friendly face.

195
00:08:22,868 --> 00:08:23,808
I explained what happened.

196
00:08:24,528 --> 00:08:26,048
He shook his head and
laughed a little, not

197
00:08:26,048 --> 00:08:28,208
in a mean way, and said I wasn't the first

198
00:08:28,348 --> 00:08:29,508
and wouldn't be the last.

199
00:08:30,268 --> 00:08:33,268
He went back to his car and came
back with two bottles of water.

200
00:08:33,688 --> 00:08:37,128
Then he leaned down to my window,
and his voice changed a little.

201
00:08:37,128 --> 00:08:40,908
He said, listen, keep your doors
locked until the tow gets here.

202
00:08:41,328 --> 00:08:43,488
If anybody walks up to
the car, you don't roll

203
00:08:43,488 --> 00:08:45,808
the window down, you call 911, and you

204
00:08:45,808 --> 00:08:47,088
tell them Deputy Harlan is out here.

205
00:08:47,968 --> 00:08:50,408
I asked him if something
had happened out here.

206
00:08:51,148 --> 00:08:53,568
He looked down the road for
a second, then back at me.

207
00:08:54,108 --> 00:08:57,208
He said, people walk this
road at night sometimes.

208
00:08:57,848 --> 00:08:59,048
I don't stop for them anymore.

209
00:08:59,888 --> 00:09:03,408
Then he told me to have a good night,
got back in his cruiser, and pulled away.

210
00:09:04,188 --> 00:09:07,348
I watched his taillights
for a long time until they

211
00:09:07,348 --> 00:09:09,968
turned into one red dot and then nothing.

212
00:09:11,128 --> 00:09:13,648
After that, the quiet felt different.

213
00:09:14,568 --> 00:09:16,768
I locked the doors and checked them twice.

214
00:09:17,248 --> 00:09:20,248
I sat with my phone in my lap and
kept looking in the side mirrors.

215
00:09:20,728 --> 00:09:23,588
The road behind me was a straight
black line under the stars.

216
00:09:23,808 --> 00:09:27,828
I could see maybe a quarter mile back,
and then it disappeared into the dark.

217
00:09:28,608 --> 00:09:31,128
I told myself the deputy
was just being careful,

218
00:09:31,148 --> 00:09:32,788
and he probably said that to everyone.

219
00:09:33,768 --> 00:09:36,348
I tried playing a podcast,
but I turned it off

220
00:09:36,348 --> 00:09:38,208
after a minute because I didn't like not

221
00:09:38,208 --> 00:09:39,008
being able to hear.

222
00:09:39,788 --> 00:09:43,048
The tow truck showed up about
an hour after the deputy left.

223
00:09:43,528 --> 00:09:45,548
I almost cried when I
saw the amber lights.

224
00:09:46,148 --> 00:09:49,768
The driver backed up in front of my
car, got out, and gave me a wave.

225
00:09:50,188 --> 00:09:53,648
He was a big guy in a reflective
vest, probably in his fifties.

226
00:09:54,008 --> 00:09:54,848
His name was Manny.

227
00:09:55,788 --> 00:09:57,648
He told me to stay in the
car while he hooked it up.

228
00:09:57,648 --> 00:09:58,748
It would only take a few minutes.

229
00:09:59,188 --> 00:10:00,768
I nodded and stayed put.

230
00:10:01,388 --> 00:10:04,068
I was watching him in
my headlights, crouched

231
00:10:04,068 --> 00:10:06,248
down at the front of my car, working with

232
00:10:06,248 --> 00:10:07,048
the chains.

233
00:10:07,308 --> 00:10:09,828
And then I looked in my rearview mirror.

234
00:10:10,848 --> 00:10:13,308
There was a woman standing behind my car.

235
00:10:14,248 --> 00:10:15,608
She wasn't on the road.

236
00:10:16,128 --> 00:10:19,368
She was on the shoulder,
maybe ten feet back, at

237
00:10:19,368 --> 00:10:21,368
the edge of the red glow
from my taillights.

238
00:10:21,948 --> 00:10:27,908
She was older, maybe sixties, thin, with
long gray hair and a hiking backpack.

239
00:10:28,748 --> 00:10:31,008
She wasn't wearing a coat,
and it was cold enough

240
00:10:31,008 --> 00:10:32,568
that I had the heat on until the battery

241
00:10:32,568 --> 00:10:33,368
scared me.

242
00:10:33,788 --> 00:10:35,288
I hadn't seen any headlights.

243
00:10:35,868 --> 00:10:37,488
I hadn't heard footsteps on the gravel.

244
00:10:38,108 --> 00:10:39,488
She was just there.

245
00:10:40,088 --> 00:10:41,168
I didn't move.

246
00:10:41,568 --> 00:10:42,848
I watched her in the mirror.

247
00:10:43,548 --> 00:10:46,048
She walked up along the
passenger side of the

248
00:10:46,048 --> 00:10:48,248
car, slowly, and stopped at the back door.

249
00:10:49,088 --> 00:10:51,848
Then she reached out
and pulled the handle.

250
00:10:53,048 --> 00:10:53,848
It was locked.

251
00:10:54,308 --> 00:10:57,328
She pulled it again, harder, and
the whole car rocked a little.

252
00:10:57,848 --> 00:11:00,028
Then she bent down and looked
through the back window.

253
00:11:00,848 --> 00:11:01,908
She wasn't looking at me.

254
00:11:02,148 --> 00:11:05,548
She was looking at the back seat, like
she expected someone to be sitting there.

255
00:11:06,368 --> 00:11:07,368
I hit the horn.

256
00:11:08,068 --> 00:11:08,868
I don't know why.

257
00:11:09,128 --> 00:11:10,828
It was the only thing I could think of.

258
00:11:11,788 --> 00:11:13,028
Manny stood up in front of the car.

259
00:11:13,548 --> 00:11:16,048
The woman straightened up
and walked to my window,

260
00:11:16,368 --> 00:11:18,168
the passenger side, and knocked on

261
00:11:18,168 --> 00:11:20,508
the glass with one knuckle, very politely.

262
00:11:21,688 --> 00:11:23,248
She leaned down and said,

263
00:11:23,488 --> 00:11:24,508
My car's just up the road.

264
00:11:24,748 --> 00:11:25,548
I've got a flat.

265
00:11:25,588 --> 00:11:27,108
I just need a ride to it.

266
00:11:27,608 --> 00:11:29,248
Her voice was normal.

267
00:11:29,908 --> 00:11:31,348
That's what I keep coming back to.

268
00:11:31,748 --> 00:11:36,708
It was a completely normal voice, a little
hoarse, like somebody's grandmother.

269
00:11:37,628 --> 00:11:38,508
I shook my head.

270
00:11:39,448 --> 00:11:40,548
She smiled at me and said,

271
00:11:40,988 --> 00:11:45,068
It's right up there and pointed down the
highway, in the direction I'd been driving.

272
00:11:46,668 --> 00:11:48,848
Manny was walking around the
front of my car now, saying,

273
00:11:48,948 --> 00:11:50,108
Hey, hey, ma'am.

274
00:11:50,668 --> 00:11:52,448
I looked at him for maybe a second.

275
00:11:52,968 --> 00:11:55,008
When I looked back at the
window, she wasn't there.

276
00:11:55,948 --> 00:11:57,828
I turned all the way around in my seat.

277
00:11:58,248 --> 00:11:59,148
The shoulder was empty.

278
00:11:59,388 --> 00:12:00,228
The road was empty.

279
00:12:01,088 --> 00:12:03,128
Manny walked all the way around
my car with his flashlight.

280
00:12:03,548 --> 00:12:05,908
He walked back along the
shoulder maybe fifty yards.

281
00:12:05,908 --> 00:12:08,068
There was nothing out there.

282
00:12:08,668 --> 00:12:09,708
No footprints that we could see.

283
00:12:10,168 --> 00:12:10,968
No sound.

284
00:12:11,048 --> 00:12:11,848
Nothing.

285
00:12:11,828 --> 00:12:13,388
The desert was flat for miles.

286
00:12:13,748 --> 00:12:15,448
There wasn't anywhere for a person to go.

287
00:12:16,448 --> 00:12:18,808
He came back to my window
and asked if I was okay.

288
00:12:19,328 --> 00:12:23,188
I told him what she'd said, that her
car was just up the road with a flat.

289
00:12:24,288 --> 00:12:25,608
He didn't say anything for a second.

290
00:12:26,288 --> 00:12:28,448
Then he asked me to ride
up in the truck with him.

291
00:12:28,948 --> 00:12:30,928
And he didn't really make
it sound like a question.

292
00:12:31,828 --> 00:12:34,608
We drove for about twenty minutes
before either of us talked.

293
00:12:35,308 --> 00:12:36,608
We passed the place where she'd pointed.

294
00:12:37,128 --> 00:12:38,828
We passed the next five miles after that.

295
00:12:39,388 --> 00:12:41,008
There was no car on the side of that road.

296
00:12:41,528 --> 00:12:42,328
There was nothing.

297
00:12:43,228 --> 00:12:44,268
Finally, Manny said,

298
00:12:44,628 --> 00:12:45,968
Did she tell you it was a flat?

299
00:12:46,488 --> 00:12:47,368
I said yes.

300
00:12:47,748 --> 00:12:49,708
He nodded and kept his eyes on the road.

301
00:12:50,288 --> 00:12:51,088
He said,

302
00:12:50,868 --> 00:12:53,028
You're the third one this
year, she's told that to.

303
00:12:53,568 --> 00:12:54,868
The other two opened the door.

304
00:12:55,728 --> 00:12:56,988
I asked him what happened to them.

305
00:12:57,448 --> 00:12:58,468
He didn't answer me.

306
00:12:58,468 --> 00:12:59,828
He just turned the radio on.

307
00:13:08,095 --> 00:13:10,515
The summer after my
freshman year of college, I

308
00:13:10,515 --> 00:13:12,555
drove home alone from
Denver to Indianapolis.

309
00:13:13,215 --> 00:13:16,815
It's about 16 hours, and I'd convinced my
parents I could do it in one day if I left

310
00:13:16,815 --> 00:13:19,635
early enough. My dad
made me promise to text

311
00:13:19,635 --> 00:13:21,735
him every time I stopped
for gas. I thought

312
00:13:21,855 --> 00:13:24,555
he was being dramatic.
That was 19, and I'd

313
00:13:24,555 --> 00:13:26,275
never had anything bad happen to me, which

314
00:13:26,275 --> 00:13:28,355
I think is the only reason
anybody is ever that confident.

315
00:13:30,015 --> 00:13:32,655
I left at five in the
morning. Everything went

316
00:13:32,655 --> 00:13:34,935
fine through eastern Colorado and most

317
00:13:34,935 --> 00:13:37,735
of Kansas. I listened
to an entire audiobook.

318
00:13:38,615 --> 00:13:40,795
I stopped for gas outside of Salina around

319
00:13:40,795 --> 00:13:43,075
six in the evening, texted
my dad a picture of

320
00:13:43,075 --> 00:13:45,315
the pump like I'd
promised, bought a coffee

321
00:13:45,315 --> 00:13:47,535
and a bag of beef jerky,
and got back on I-70.

322
00:13:48,535 --> 00:13:50,955
By the time it got dark,
I was somewhere in eastern

323
00:13:50,955 --> 00:13:52,915
Kansas, and the traffic had thinned

324
00:13:52,915 --> 00:13:55,975
out to almost nothing. Just
me and the occasional semi.

325
00:13:57,335 --> 00:14:00,635
That's when I noticed
the headlights. They were

326
00:14:00,635 --> 00:14:03,155
behind me. Not close. Maybe a few hundred

327
00:14:03,155 --> 00:14:06,195
feet back. At first I
didn't think about it,

328
00:14:06,195 --> 00:14:08,075
but I was driving a little under the limit

329
00:14:08,075 --> 00:14:10,215
because I was tired, and
normally people would

330
00:14:10,215 --> 00:14:13,795
pass me. These didn't. When I slowed down,

331
00:14:14,055 --> 00:14:16,335
they slowed down. When
I sped up to see what

332
00:14:16,335 --> 00:14:19,135
happened, they sped up, and kept the exact

333
00:14:19,135 --> 00:14:22,475
same distance, like they were
attached to my car on a string.

334
00:14:23,375 --> 00:14:29,495
I changed lanes. A few seconds later, so
did they. I changed back. So did they.

335
00:14:30,135 --> 00:14:33,335
I told myself it was a
coincidence, that somebody

336
00:14:33,335 --> 00:14:35,735
else was just tired and
following my taillights,

337
00:14:35,935 --> 00:14:39,595
which people do. But it
kept going. Twenty minutes.

338
00:14:40,195 --> 00:14:43,135
Forty minutes. An hour. Every time

339
00:14:43,135 --> 00:14:45,275
I checked the mirror, they
were in the same spot.

340
00:14:45,955 --> 00:14:48,175
Two round headlights, a little dimmer

341
00:14:48,175 --> 00:14:48,975
on the left side.

342
00:14:49,515 --> 00:14:52,435
I called my dad. I
tried to sound calm, but

343
00:14:52,435 --> 00:14:59,475
I don't think I did. He told me not to get

344
00:14:59,475 --> 00:15:02,175
right up front. I opened
my map, and there was

345
00:15:02,175 --> 00:15:03,575
a truck stop about twelve miles ahead.

346
00:15:04,515 --> 00:15:06,095
He stayed on the phone
with me the whole way.

347
00:15:06,795 --> 00:15:08,455
Every few minutes he asked, are they still

348
00:15:08,455 --> 00:15:10,295
there? And every time I said yes.

349
00:15:11,135 --> 00:15:14,655
The truck stop was huge.
Rows of semis in the

350
00:15:14,655 --> 00:15:16,975
back. A restaurant. A convenience store.

351
00:15:17,755 --> 00:15:20,475
Bright white lights over
everything. I pulled into

352
00:15:20,475 --> 00:15:22,735
a spot right in front of the store, close

353
00:15:22,735 --> 00:15:25,995
enough that I could see the cashier
through the window. I watched my mirror.

354
00:15:26,695 --> 00:15:31,675
The car pulled in a few seconds later. It
drove slowly past the front of the store,

355
00:15:32,335 --> 00:15:34,875
past me, and all the way
to the far end of the

356
00:15:34,875 --> 00:15:36,595
lot, where the lights didn't reach very

357
00:15:36,255 --> 00:15:40,955
well. It parked facing my direction.
Then its headlights turned off.

358
00:15:42,355 --> 00:15:43,535
Nobody got out.

359
00:15:44,255 --> 00:15:47,255
I sat there for ten
minutes with my dad on the

360
00:15:47,255 --> 00:15:49,815
phone. The car didn't move. I couldn't

361
00:15:49,815 --> 00:15:52,815
see what kind of car it
was. Just a dark shape.

362
00:15:53,155 --> 00:15:55,935
Maybe a sedan? I couldn't
see anyone inside

363
00:15:55,935 --> 00:15:58,715
it. My dad said to go
inside and tell someone,

364
00:15:58,895 --> 00:16:00,815
so I did. I almost ran into the store.

365
00:16:01,835 --> 00:16:04,755
The cashier was a teenage
kid who didn't really know

366
00:16:04,755 --> 00:16:07,035
what to do with me.
But there was a trucker

367
00:16:07,035 --> 00:16:09,475
at the coffee station
who'd heard me. An older

368
00:16:09,475 --> 00:16:11,895
man in a Carhartt jacket
with a white beard

369
00:16:11,895 --> 00:16:14,495
and reading glasses
pushed up on his head. He

370
00:16:14,495 --> 00:16:16,835
asked me which car. I
pointed it out through

371
00:16:16,835 --> 00:16:19,535
the window. He looked
at it for a long time.

372
00:16:20,075 --> 00:16:22,675
Then he said, okay, where are you headed?

373
00:16:23,555 --> 00:16:28,215
His name was Walt. He told me he was going
east anyway, and he'd follow right behind

374
00:16:28,215 --> 00:16:30,855
me to the next big town,
about forty miles. He

375
00:16:30,855 --> 00:16:32,615
said if that car tried anything, it would

376
00:16:32,615 --> 00:16:34,635
have to deal with eighty
thousand pounds of truck

377
00:16:34,635 --> 00:16:36,975
first. He gave me his CB channel, but

378
00:16:36,975 --> 00:16:39,435
I didn't have a CB, so
we just traded phone

379
00:16:39,435 --> 00:16:41,295
numbers. My dad talked to him on my phone

380
00:16:41,295 --> 00:16:43,555
for a minute. I don't
know what they said, but

381
00:16:43,555 --> 00:16:45,615
after that, my dad sounded a lot calmer.

382
00:16:46,375 --> 00:16:49,675
We pulled out together.
My car, then Walt's

383
00:16:49,675 --> 00:16:52,915
truck, then nothing. I watched the far end

384
00:16:52,915 --> 00:16:55,415
of the lot as we passed,
and the dark car was

385
00:16:55,415 --> 00:16:57,175
still sitting there with its lights off.

386
00:16:58,115 --> 00:17:01,075
About five miles down the
highway, the headlights

387
00:17:01,075 --> 00:17:04,675
came back. They came up
fast this time, much

388
00:17:04,675 --> 00:17:07,275
faster than before. I saw
them grow in my mirror

389
00:17:07,275 --> 00:17:09,735
past Walt's truck, and I felt my whole

390
00:17:09,735 --> 00:17:13,635
body go cold. Then Walt
moved. He swung his truck

391
00:17:13,635 --> 00:17:15,135
into the left lane, right next to the

392
00:17:15,135 --> 00:17:19,615
car, and stayed there, matching its speed.
Every time I tried to get around him, he

393
00:17:19,615 --> 00:17:24,655
drifted over and blocked it. He called me
and said, very calmly, just keep driving,

394
00:17:24,775 --> 00:17:29,575
sweetheart. Don't speed. I've got him. It
went on for maybe two or three minutes.

395
00:17:29,995 --> 00:17:32,935
Then the car braked
hard, dropped back, and

396
00:17:32,935 --> 00:17:35,795
took the next exit. Walt stayed behind me

397
00:17:35,795 --> 00:17:38,175
for the next forty miles.
He followed me into

398
00:17:38,175 --> 00:17:39,795
the parking lot of a police station in the

399
00:17:39,795 --> 00:17:43,175
next town, waited while
I went in, and then he

400
00:17:43,175 --> 00:17:44,815
flashed his lights twice and pulled back

401
00:17:44,815 --> 00:17:48,915
onto the highway. I never saw him
again. I wish I'd gotten his last name.

402
00:17:49,555 --> 00:17:52,515
I told the officer at
the desk everything. I

403
00:17:52,515 --> 00:17:54,115
didn't have a plate number, and I didn't

404
00:17:54,115 --> 00:17:56,495
really expect anything
to come of it. He took

405
00:17:56,495 --> 00:17:59,255
my information, gave me a coffee, and let

406
00:17:59,255 --> 00:18:01,155
me sit in the lobby until
I felt okay to drive the

407
00:18:01,155 --> 00:18:03,615
last few hours home.
My dad met me halfway.

408
00:18:04,895 --> 00:18:07,415
Two days later, a detective
from Kansas called

409
00:18:07,415 --> 00:18:10,455
me. He said a patrol car had found a dark

410
00:18:10,455 --> 00:18:12,875
gray sedan abandoned at
that same truck stop

411
00:18:12,875 --> 00:18:14,955
the next morning, parked at the far end of

412
00:18:14,955 --> 00:18:18,555
the lot. The car had been
reported stolen four

413
00:18:18,555 --> 00:18:20,315
days earlier from a woman in Colorado.

414
00:18:20,915 --> 00:18:25,655
The woman had also been reported missing.
He asked me a lot of questions about what

415
00:18:25,655 --> 00:18:31,235
I saw, and I didn't have many answers. Then
he asked me if I'd bought gas in Salina

416
00:18:31,235 --> 00:18:34,595
that evening. I said
yes. I'd texted my dad

417
00:18:34,595 --> 00:18:36,355
a picture of the pump, so I even knew the

418
00:18:36,595 --> 00:18:41,455
exact time. He said they'd found a receipt
on the passenger seat of that car. It was

419
00:18:41,455 --> 00:18:43,635
from the same gas station
in Salina, Pump 4,

420
00:18:43,635 --> 00:18:47,115
the pump next to mine. It was printed one

421
00:18:47,595 --> 00:18:48,675
minute after my receipt.

